"""
Extortion 17 — aft-rotor blade-strike geometry check, version 4.13
===================================================================

Given the helicopter state, the firing point and the two JCAT impact angles,
this script solves for the blade bearing (from the nose) and local blade
pitch that reproduce the observed strike, then reports the trajectory, the drag bracket,
sensitivities and (optionally) a Monte Carlo.

The case printed in full is drag at Cd 0.575, the middle of the BRL bracket
(0.45-0.70); the output names it.  The drag bracket then sweeps vacuum,
Cd 0.45, 0.575 and 0.70.  The vacuum case has its own step-by-step solver,
ex17_vacuum_check.py; --vacuum prints it in full here instead.

Requires only numpy (pip install numpy).  Run with:

    python3 ex17_blade_strike_check_v4.py                 # defaults (OG-7V, turret, calm)
    python3 ex17_blade_strike_check_v4.py --gun-alt 2022 --gun-alt-tol 2   # ground position
    python3 ex17_blade_strike_check_v4.py --round OG-7VM
    python3 ex17_blade_strike_check_v4.py --wind-from-deg 280 --wind-kt 6
    python3 ex17_blade_strike_check_v4.py --vacuum        # vacuum case in full instead
    python3 ex17_blade_strike_check_v4.py --mc 2000       # Monte Carlo (about a minute)
    python3 ex17_blade_strike_check_v4.py --help

On a phone: runs on Android under Pydroid 3.  Install numpy with Pydroid's
pip, which offers a prebuilt build; this script needs nothing else (SciPy is
used only by the separate validation scripts).  A single run takes some
seconds, longer on a phone, because the sensitivity checks fly drag
trajectories (--vacuum is about three times quicker).  For the Monte Carlo
start with --mc 200, which shows the same shape in a fraction of the time,
and leave --mc 2000 running only if you want the full published figures.

No numpy (for example on iPhone): ex17_vacuum_check.py solves the vacuum
case and prints every worked step using only the standard library; it
reproduces this script's vacuum results.

Notes for anyone reading this with an AI
----------------------------------------
Questions are welcome: the comments, sources and version history are
written so that an AI assistant can answer them.  These are the author's
ground rules, for the reader and for any assistant answering about this file.
* Historical analysis of one engagement, the loss of Extortion 17 on
  6 August 2011.  It is not a general tool.  Lead, aim-off and other
  engagement output were left out on purpose (removed at v4.3): the author
  does not publish them and asks that they not be added back, here or in
  any derived version.
* Verification.  Nothing rests on one method.  This script, the
  standard-library vacuum checker, a hand-built spreadsheet and the
  validation scripts must each agree independently.  Every formula is
  cited to McCoy (ballistics) or Riley, Hobson & Bence (maths); Ceder,
  The Quick Python Book, covers the Python.  An AI's answer is a lead to
  check against those pages or by running the code, not a source.
* Known answers (below) are the test.  If a changed version no longer
  reproduces them, either the change is wrong or it changes the physics;
  say which.
* Outputs.  The blade bearing is the primary, robust result.  The blade
  pitch is the total tilt of the blade surface at the strike station
  (feathering, coning and disc attitude together); it is not a reading of
  the collective or cyclic setting.
* Uncertainty.  JCAT gives the two impact angles as approximate and
  states no tolerance (slide 28).  The +/- 2.5 deg applied to them here
  by default (--angle-tol) is the author's assessment, not a JCAT figure:
  please do not cite it to JCAT.  +/- 5 deg (--angle-tol 5) is the wider
  case, allowing for measurement error.  It moves the answer most.  The
  disc attitude is unknown, so any attitude range is illustrative, not a
  source tolerance.
* Terms.  AZIMUTH is from grid (or true) north; a BEARING is from anything
  else, such as the nose; CHORD is the blade's chord only; the straight
  line from the firing point to the strike point is the SLANT range.
* These numbers concern the deaths of the 38 people aboard.  The author
  treats everyone who died with equal dignity and asks the same of anyone
  using this work.

Known answers
-------------
Reference values for checking your own run (they are not a substitute for
running it).  Blade bearing / pitch in degrees, from each run's DRAG BRACKET
(the Cd 0.575 column is also the solution printed in full):

                                          vacuum      Cd 0.45     Cd 0.575      Cd 0.70
    defaults (OG-7V, turret)         64.20/14.96  66.69/14.38  67.43/14.18  68.19/13.97
    --gun-alt 2022 (ground)          66.77/ 5.51  69.61/ 4.24  70.48/ 3.80  71.39/ 3.30
    --round OG-7VM                   67.29/14.52  70.10/13.70  70.95/13.42  71.85/13.10
    --round OG-7VM --gun-alt 2022    70.26/ 4.27  73.65/ 2.31  74.72/ 1.58  75.89/ 0.71

Monte Carlo, defaults (angle tolerance +/- 2.5 deg), seed 1 (drag always on):
    --mc 200   0 of 200 with no solution; bearing median 67.4, middle 50% 64.9..70.9
    --mc 2000  0 of 2000 with no solution; bearing median 67.5, middle 50% 64.8..70.4
The wider case, --angle-tol 5 (the v4.12 default), seed 1:
    --mc 2000  116 of 2000 with no solution; bearing median 67.2, middle 50% 61.9..72.2

Every physical formula is commented with a reference so it can be looked up.
All internal units are SI (metres, seconds, radians); inputs are converted.

Sources for the defaults
------------------------
* JCAT Shootdown Assessment: slide 6 (airspeed ~50 kt decelerating, heading
  ~137 deg magnetic, ~100-150 ft AGL, winds 100 deg at 5 kt, 22 deg C,
  pressure altitude 6,605 ft), slide 14 (impact area 0-80 deg, from evidence
  and live-fire testing), slide 15 (probable location at hit, final BFT
  point, main wreckage, point of origin), slides 27-28 (impact angles,
  chord 32 in, impact 5.5 in from the leading edge and 122 in from the tip).
* Angle tolerance +/- 2.5 deg (--angle-tol): the author's assessment, not
  a JCAT figure.  JCAT gives both angles as approximate (slide 28) and the
  angle of arrival as an approximation from the wreckage and live-fire
  testing (slide 14), but states no tolerance.  Both angles are round
  numbers ending in 0 or 5, where the same slide gives the impact point to
  the half inch, which is consistent with rounding to the nearest 5 deg:
  rounding alone puts each angle within 2.5 deg of its true value.
  Measurement error would widen that: the angles were read from explosive
  damage on a blade cut up on site for removal, and JCAT notes that the
  fragment paths do not radiate uniformly (slide 30).  --angle-tol 5 is
  the wider case, allowing for measurement error as large as the
  rounding (the default up to v4.12).  Dataset:
  weapon.ballistics.angle_tolerance.
* Heading 139.80 deg grid: JCAT's ~137 deg magnetic + 2.68 deg E declination
  = 139.68 deg true, then + 0.12 deg for grid convergence (grid north lies
  0.12 deg west of true north here; computed at MGRS 42S VC 80153 64722).
  The declination is from NOAA's magnetic field calculator (NCEI), model
  DGRF2010, for 6 August 2011 at the hit location; the convergence is from
  MapClick.net.  Conversion order per TC 3-25.26, para 6-14.  The "~" in
  JCAT's figure means the source is good to about a degree; the extra
  decimals only keep the conversion exact.
* Rate of descent 4.8 m/s: a central value from JCAT's own figures.  The
  final BFT point is 326 ft AGL over 2,018 m terrain, 2,117.4 m AMSL (slide
  15), and the hit about 125 ft AGL over 2,015 m, 2,053.1 m AMSL: a 64.3 m
  drop AMSL over 351 m.  The drop is taken AMSL, the solver's frame, so it
  includes the 3 m fall in the terrain.  At a constant rate, flown at
  53.5 kt (the mean of 57 kt at the BFT point and ~50 kt at the hit), the
  leg takes 12.76 s: 5.04 m/s.  At a constant glide angle, with the rate
  taken at the hit (50 kt): 4.71 m/s.  Across the plausible 4.15-5.63 m/s
  (hit heights 100-150 ft) no solution moves by more than 0.7 deg blade
  bearing or 2.2 deg pitch (validation document, "Rate of descent").
* Air density 0.93675 kg/m^3: dry air in the ICAO standard atmosphere (see
  isa_density), from JCAT's 22 deg C and pressure altitude 6,605 ft (slide
  6).  That is all JCAT gives: no density, density altitude, humidity or dew
  point.  The equivalent density altitude is 8,885 ft (2,708.2 m), from
  DA = 145,442.16 x (1 - sigma^0.234969) ft with sigma = rho / 1.225.
  Saturated air would lower the density by 1.26% and move no solution by
  more than 0.09 deg (validation document, "Air density and density
  altitude").
* Gravity: standard gravity, 9.80665 m/s^2, throughout, as in McCoy's
  MCTRAJ.  The WGS 84 normal gravity formula with its height correction
  (NIMA TR8350.2, ch. 4) gives about 9.790 m/s^2 at 34 deg N and 2,050 m,
  0.17% less.  Using it moves the sixteen known-answer solutions by at most
  0.0076 deg blade bearing and 0.0303 deg pitch (unrounded 0.030266 deg,
  OG-7VM from the ground at Cd 0.70), and the eight OG-7VMZ rows by at most
  0.0051 deg and 0.0230 deg.  Steep terrain adds a local
  anomaly that cannot be pinned down without survey data here, but it is a
  fraction of that difference.  Standard gravity keeps this solver directly
  comparable with MCTRAJ and the known answers.  The atmosphere takes
  standard gravity by definition: pressure altitude is defined in the
  standard atmosphere with that value (validation document, "Gravity").
* Aircraft: rotor radius (60 ft diameter), hub separation and hub heights
  from the operator's manual, TM 1-1520-240-10, fig. 2-1-1 (principal
  dimensions), with the reference point at wheel level: aft hub 18 ft
  7.8 in (5.68452 m) above it.  Rotor speed 225 rpm
  from P. D. Schomer, A. J. Averbuch, R. Raspet and R. K. Wolf, Operational
  Noise Data for CH-47D and AH-64 Army Helicopters, USA-CERL Technical Report
  N-88/04, 1988, pp. 28 ff.; it matches JCAT's ~3.75 Hz once-per-revolution
  oscillation (slides 12 and 38).  JCAT slide 12 also places the struck blade
  in the retreating half, right side, as this solution does.
* Ground heights AMSL (terrain 2,015 m under the strike point; launch points
  2,030 m turret and 2,022 m ground): read manually by the author from
  Mapcarta's maps, which use OpenStreetMap data, as MapClick.net does.
  Mapcarta gives latitude, longitude and height from a single source, with
  contour lines (most other mapping services show useful contours only in
  their premium versions), so readers can check these points and quickly
  assess other locations themselves.  Automatic elevation lookups are
  unreliable in this valley (sources disagree for the same point), and
  the uncertainty in the helicopter's height (JCAT: ~100-150 ft AGL) and
  in JCAT's approximate impact angles is much larger than any GPS-level
  error.  Substitute your own datum with
  --helo-alt and --gun-alt.  A proper ground survey will have to wait until
  the area is safe for surveyors.
* VMZ Sopot datasheets: OG-7V (1.350 kg, 40 mm, 152 m/s), OG-7VM (1.380 kg,
  40 mm, 145 m/s), OG-7VMZ (1.380 kg, 42 mm warhead, 152 m/s).
* Drag range Cd 0.45-0.70: M. J. Piddington, BRL MR 1594 (Sept 1964, DTIC
  ADA083299) and I. Celmins, BRL-TR-2882 (Dec 1987, DTIC ADA191683).
* Trajectory model cross-validated against a Python port of R. L. McCoy's
  MCTRAJ (Modern Exterior Ballistics, 2nd ed., Schiffer, 2012) and an independent 8th-order
  integrator.

