Limits of the method
--------------------
* Coning is NOT modelled.  The blade is treated as lying in the rotor disc
  plane; feathering is the only blade rotation applied (about the span axis).
  Coning tilts the chord plane in the same sense as leading-edge-up
  feathering as seen by a round arriving from below; each degree of coning
  removes about four degrees of the feathering the solver would otherwise
  demand, while the blade bearing barely moves.  The reported "blade pitch" is
  therefore the total tilt of the blade surface at the strike station.
* Blade twist, flapping velocity and shaft tilt are not modelled; the disc is
  level unless --pitch-att / --roll say otherwise (JCAT: attitude unknown).
* The strike point is placed on the blade's pitch axis; JCAT puts it 5.5 in
  aft of the leading edge, which moves the aim point ~14 cm (negligible).
* UTM grid offsets are used directly as east/north, and the heading is a
  GRID azimuth (JCAT's magnetic heading converted with declination and grid
  convergence, about 0.12 deg here), so the whole calculation stays in the
  grid frame.  Azimuths are reported in grid and converted to true only for
  display.  The wind direction is applied as given (true); the 0.12 deg
  difference is ignored (default calm).
* Point-mass trajectory: no yaw, spin or Coriolis; Cd constant below Mach 1.

Coordinate frames and conventions
---------------------------------
ENU   : local East / North / Up, metres, origin at the helicopter centre point.
        East and North are UTM GRID axes, because the positions come from MGRS.
BODY  : forward / starboard / up, obtained from heading (note: this triad is
        LEFT-handed; cross products are never taken in body coordinates).
BLADE : span (tip -> root), chord (leading -> trailing edge), normal (bottom -> top).
* Terms.  An AZIMUTH is a direction measured clockwise from grid north (from
  true north where marked T or true), as in land navigation.  The STANDARD
  ROTOR AZIMUTH psi is the aviation term: measured from the tail, in the
  direction of rotation.  A BEARING is measured from anything else: the
  BLADE BEARING is measured from the NOSE, clockwise (toward starboard).
  For a clockwise rotor psi = blade bearing + 180 deg.
* CHORD means only the blade's chord, the direction across the blade from
  leading to trailing edge.  The straight-line distance from the firing
  point to the strike point is the SLANT range.
* In the code the blade bearing is called az (a historical name).
* Blade pitch is positive leading-edge-up.
* The aft rotor of a CH-47 turns clockwise when viewed from above.
"""

import argparse
import math
import numpy as np

G = 9.80665           # standard gravity, m/s^2 (ISO 80000-3); MCTRAJ uses 32.174 ft/s^2, the same value
FT = 0.3048           # international foot, m
IN = 0.0254           # international inch, m
KT = 1852.0 / 3600.0  # knot, m/s
R_AIR = 287.053       # specific gas constant of dry air, J/(kg K) (ICAO Doc 7488)

ROUNDS = {            # VMZ Sopot datasheets; mass and calibre are the grenade in flight
    "OG-7V":   dict(projectile="OG-7",   v0=152.0, mass=1.350, dia=0.040),
